#{[17592186149844 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186149846] [17592186149860 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186149862] [17592186149865 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186149867] [17592186149868 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186149870] [17592186150173 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186150177] [17592186151907 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 6x-5x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(6x\\operatorname{ln}x+5x)-5x(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}x+{x}^{2})+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x & =0 \\\\ (6{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x+5{x}^{3}-15{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x-5{x}^{3}+9{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{x}^{3} & {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2})-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{5}-3{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."] [17592186152565 :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{x,\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{x},\\hspace{0.167em}2x\\}$ defined on $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}."] [17592186152565 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions $\\{{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)\\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$"] [17592186152566 :question/worked-solution "The relation $k_1+k_2\\sin x=0$ must hold for every real $x$. At $x=0$ it gives $k_1=0$. At $x=\\pi/6$ it gives\n\n$$\nk_1+\\frac12k_2=0.\n$$\n\nCombining these equations gives $k_2=0$. Thus only the trivial coefficients work, so $1$ and $\\sin x$ are linearly independent."] [17592186152567 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186391201] [17592186152567 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186391204] [17592186152567 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186391207] [17592186152567 :question/problem "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\hspace{2em}{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\hspace{2em}x\\in \\mathbb{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186152567 :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\{{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)\\}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $\\mathbb{R}.$"] [17592186152568 :question/worked-solution "Differentiate $y_1=\\cos(2x)$ and $y_2=\\sin(2x)$, then compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(y_1,y_2)&=y_1y_2^{\\prime}-y_2y_1^{\\prime}\\\\&=\\cos(2x)\\cdot2\\cos(2x)-\\sin(2x)\\cdot(-2\\sin(2x))\\\\&=2(\\cos^2(2x)+\\sin^2(2x))\\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is nonzero for every real $x$, so the functions are linearly independent."] [17592186152569 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}(x)+\\operatorname{sin}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in \\mathbb{R},$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation”; “${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186152570 :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0{\\color{darkgreen}\\hspace{1em}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0{\\color{red}\\hspace{1em}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement “The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation” is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation” only."] [17592186152611 :question/worked-solution "Using $W(y_1,y_2)=y_1y_2^{\\prime}-y_2y_1^{\\prime}$, we get\n\n$$\nW=(x^2-1)\\cdot1-x\\cdot2x=-x^2-1.\n$$"] [17592186164163 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164166] [17592186164339 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164340] [17592186164344 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164348] [17592186166533 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\ln 2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2(12)+1(4) \\\\ 1(12)+2(4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}24+4 \\\\ 12+8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}28 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186167191 :question/worked-solution "Follow the arrows from the marked point. The trajectory initially moves upward and to the right. It reaches the steeper dashed nullcline with a vertical tangent, turns left, and then crosses the other dashed nullcline with a horizontal tangent. Afterward it bends downward and left. The matching trajectory is\n\n![](images/67/67273ae9c0f9d50f1a95da3c61039fa9cf262a7b34b93cc9e6f1fe26cd463a5f.png)"] [17592186167194 :question/problem "$$\n{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}(t),\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{x}(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{x}(t)={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2?$"] [17592186167194 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & ={e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}2)}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}^{'}(\\operatorname{ln}2) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{59}{4} \\\\ \\frac{11}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\left(\\frac{59}{4}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2\\left(\\frac{59}{4}\\right)-3\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{37}{2} \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186167195 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186390897] [17592186167195 :question/answer-fields #edb/ref 17592186390902] [17592186167216 :question/worked-solution "On the $x$-nullcline, $x^{\\prime}=0$, so\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=-3x.\n$$\n\nOn the $y$-nullcline, $y^{\\prime}=0$, so\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y=\\frac12x.\n$$"]}
